title: Regular expressions in queries
tags: Query
kind: reference
aliases: matches match
summary: MATCHES runs a case-sensitive, bounded regex over a field's distinct values; a tree_ref needs :value or :path.

`field MATCHES "pattern"` tests a regular expression (the `regex` crate) against the
field's text. It is ''case-sensitive''; `(?i)` at the front of the pattern ignores
case (`osm`/`osmd` are always case-insensitive). It is unanchored: `"jazz"` matches
anywhere, `"^jazz$"` the whole text.

On a `tree_ref` it needs an aspect ([[Field aspects]]): `field:value MATCHES …` tests
the node's own name, `field:path MATCHES …` its whole path.

Matching takes linear time — the engine never backtracks — and a pattern's compiled
form is capped at 1 MiB (`regexp.rs`, shared with `mf_ignore` and the test oracle, so
the forms cannot diverge). A pattern that does not compile, or is too large, is
refused with `400` before evaluation.

The pattern runs over the field's ''distinct values'', not its rows: a field whose
values repeat — a tag, an extension, a folder name — costs its number of distinct
values. Inside an `AND` the text operand runs last, on the running intersection, so
"this folder, name matching X" costs the folder rather than the repository. Its
literal parts first narrow the candidates by trigrams ([[Text search in the store]]).
A search with no literal and no narrowing reads every value of the field — cheap on
a small repository, not on five million files ([[Query limits]]).
